[[:db/retract 17592186395899 :answer-field/choices 17592186395904] [:db/retract 17592186395899 :answer-field/choices 17592186395900] [:db/retract 17592186395899 :answer-field/choices 17592186395901] [:db/retract 17592186395899 :answer-field/choices 17592186395902] [:db/retract 17592186395899 :answer-field/choices 17592186395903] {:db/id 17592186133778 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-adc7f30b-f5f4-5c71-84b9-9c4c7b9ab1ff"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133587 :question/problem "For the vector ![](images/c3/c32c8627f43b93123b110207a136416c9b498a9b0fbab9adc671e039839f098c.svg) which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to the set ![](images/62/621d5f7a19f100f4c7089900b4ef61851bb23ad580127a03c0050bdb295e1802.svg)\n\n- $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to the set ![](images/5e/5e3fe028385135bd1e744ae7ee0e0d6ed319bf5fc145ae70bee0b30991008964.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133588 :question/problem "Given that ![](images/60/6052e4c3f0ad4e3ddf02b9200c89e808b24a8820de6445677ef0d685927b7b80.svg) is a non-zero vector that is orthogonal to the set ![](images/c0/c09dc7dd7a9e4b4493e3c1e2c1324dffe991e8948a371c0ab9d4de9828c280a3.svg) find the value of $\\dfrac{a+c}{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "The vector $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{w}_1=0$ and $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{w}_2=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n![](images/fa/fafbc63ea1e75cd6d7f1dfc93e7e5a83d8f570c6c3a7b356f818debbb091cdfb.svg)\n\nWe write the corresponding augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd ones), so $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get $x_2=-x_3.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get $x_1=-3x_3.$ So, the general solution is\n\n![](images/a9/a9bbd5f866831a70146beb08aa61180fe7dc3aa27e10a767e5fe070de675349e.svg)\n\nSetting $x_3=1,$ we get the vector \n![](images/32/324a22d18daa5c69f63e89036b6e728a41b8d2eb5a0b13422d733b8625ec1652.svg)\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=-3,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=1.$\n\nFinally, $\\dfrac{a+c}{b}=\\dfrac{-3+1}{-1}=2.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133817 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4c6cb332-318e-5ab0-89ce-ace167bccb04"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133632 :question/problem "Consider the vector subspace ![](images/20/208ca9a2a786c6c059c9bad9a129fe386c79e48e422b5dfa7278dd74c785067e.svg) Which of the following vectors belong to $H^{\\perp}?$\n![](images/8e/8e7da4f04ac9eae56aa22a780ee5130e9b3dcf5f8adbcae139e7a415fd54ac83.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133780 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-fc37a677-a8ea-5147-882f-3adaa2a20db5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133589 :question/problem "Find $H^{\\perp}$ if ![](images/55/5511eb6c1d36976fdf7e1e9a5fdaeef489d425b227dcd0a4cbf857933c740ed0.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as $\\mathbf{h}_1$ and $\\mathbf{h}_2.$ \n\nA vector ![](images/98/986f6778009507b2fd597e22ce9427e1220932d547b5e16fa664309ab6e719ce.svg) belongs to $H^{\\perp}$ if $\\mathbf{x}\\cdot \\mathbf{h}_1=0$ and $\\mathbf{x}\\cdot \\mathbf{h}_2=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n![](images/ae/aea42e47226db466060875ea1ac0f4603c45194bbad87d431f9b5fbacaccf51a.svg) \n\nWe write the corresponding augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd ones), so $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation we get $x_2=-\\dfrac{1}{3}x_3.$ Substituting this into first equation, we obtain $x_1 = -\\dfrac{1}{3}x_3.$\n\nSo, the general solution to the system is\n![](images/e5/e5f6a6c2b497838b064a43264e85a031ad431633228401507a599167cc970c5c.svg)\n\nTherefore, our orthogonal complement is\n![](images/6e/6e3de0d6e9be6bc505c6aa37ab412a6a51915e08cf7bf0d34a33ec79aa6d9181.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134227 :question/problem "For the vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $u$ is orthogonal to the set $H=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nII. $u$ is orthogonal to the set $V=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nIII. $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $H,V$ and $W$ as $\\{{h}_{1},{h}_{2}\\},\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ and $\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{2}\\}$, respectively. \n\nNow, we have to compute the dot product between $u$ and the vectors in each set. \n\n- For the set $H:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {h}_{1} & =1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-2)=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {h}_{2} & =1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot (-1)=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u$ is orthogonal to the set $H.$\n\n- For the set $V:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {v}_{1} & =1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-3)=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {v}_{2} & =1\\cdot 3+1\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot (-1)=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {v}_{3} & =1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-3)+1\\cdot (-1)=-2\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u$ is *not* orthogonal to the set $V.$\n\n- For the set $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} & =1\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 4+1\\cdot (-3)=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} & =1\\cdot (2)+1\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1=2\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u$ is *not* orthogonal to the set $W.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-adc7f30b-f5f4-5c71-84b9-9c4c7b9ab1ff" :question/id #uuid "adc7f30b-f5f4-5c71-84b9-9c4c7b9ab1ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-140464" :question/problem "For the vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $u⊥H,\\,$ where $H=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nII. $u⊥V,\\,$ where $V=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nIII. $u⊥W,\\,$ where $W=\\{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $H,V$ and $W$ as $\\{{h}_{1},{h}_{2}\\},\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{2},{w}_{3}\\}$, respectively.  \n\nNow, we have to compute the dot product between $u$ and the vectors in each set. \n\n- For the set $H:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {h}_{1} & =2\\cdot (-4)+1\\cdot 10+(-2)\\cdot 1=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {h}_{2} & =2\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 10+(-2)\\cdot 5=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u⊥H.$\n\n- For the set $V:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {v}_{1} & =2\\cdot (-4)+1\\cdot 10+(-2)\\cdot (-1)=4\\ne 0 & \\times  \\\\ u\\cdot {v}_{2} & =2\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 10+(-2)\\cdot (-5)=20\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u⊥̸V.$\n\n- For the set $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} & =2\\cdot 5+1\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot 5=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} & =2\\cdot 10+1\\cdot (-5)+(-2)\\cdot 0=15\\ne 0 & \\times  \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{3} & =2\\cdot (-4)+1\\cdot 10+(-2)\\cdot (-1)=4\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u⊥̸W.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a22e3410-aef3-5a94-97ba-9cdaa238fcc3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a22e3410-aef3-5a94-97ba-9cdaa238fcc3" :answer-field/id #uuid "a22e3410-aef3-5a94-97ba-9cdaa238fcc3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3e29f639-6dea-54ac-bb7f-18bb3d312722" :answer-field/choices ["answer-35a22425-0fd6-5e91-8dbd-62be5dc9184d" "answer-3e29f639-6dea-54ac-bb7f-18bb3d312722" "answer-81f8e02c-48df-5958-a046-15ad41372d47" "answer-1e2a07a5-dbe2-541d-8ce4-ee6e5f93fe79" "answer-5bfd9fd9-87e6-5685-a09c-de3d488b9e58"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-35a22425-0fd6-5e91-8dbd-62be5dc9184d" :answer/id #uuid "35a22425-0fd6-5e91-8dbd-62be5dc9184d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3e29f639-6dea-54ac-bb7f-18bb3d312722" :answer/id #uuid "3e29f639-6dea-54ac-bb7f-18bb3d312722" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-81f8e02c-48df-5958-a046-15ad41372d47" :answer/id #uuid "81f8e02c-48df-5958-a046-15ad41372d47" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1e2a07a5-dbe2-541d-8ce4-ee6e5f93fe79" :answer/id #uuid "1e2a07a5-dbe2-541d-8ce4-ee6e5f93fe79" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5bfd9fd9-87e6-5685-a09c-de3d488b9e58" :answer/id #uuid "5bfd9fd9-87e6-5685-a09c-de3d488b9e58" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186418767 :question/problem "Given that $u=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]$ is a non-zero  vector that is orthogonal to the set $W=\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\},$ find the value of $\\frac{b+c}{a}.$" :question/worked-solution "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ⇔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{4} & -\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=2,$ we get the vector \n$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=1,$ $b=2,$ and $c=2.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b+c}{a}=\\frac{2+2}{1}=4.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134498 :question/problem "Given that $u=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]$ is a non-zero vector that is orthogonal to the set $W=\\{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\},$ find the value of $\\frac{b+c}{a}.$" :question/worked-solution "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}-3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=5{x}_{3},$ and from the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{3} \\\\ 5{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=4$, we get the vector \n$[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 20 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=3,$ $b=20,$ and $c=4.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b+c}{a}=\\frac{20+4}{3}=8.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133026 :answer-field/correct 17592186133030 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186348414 :question/problem "Consider the vector subspace $H=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\}.$ Which of the following vectors belong to ${H}^{⊥}?$\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-4c6cb332-318e-5ab0-89ce-ace167bccb04" :question/id #uuid "4c6cb332-318e-5ab0-89ce-ace167bccb04" :question/math-academy-id "q-39951" :question/problem "Consider the vector subspace $H=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\}.$ Which of the following vectors belong to ${H}^{⊥}?$\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{2}.$ \n\nNow, a vector $v$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $v$ is orthogonal to the set of vectors that spans the subspace $H.$ So, we have to find the products ${v}_{i}\\cdot {h}_{j}$ for for $i=1,2,3$ and  $j=1,2.$  \n\n- For ${v}_{1}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =(-2)\\cdot (3)+3\\cdot (-5)+1\\cdot 4=-17\\ne 0 & \\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{1}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$ We do not need to test ${v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{2}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{2}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =1\\cdot 3+3\\cdot (-5)+3\\cdot 4=0 & \\,✓ \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{2} & =1\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 2+3\\cdot (-3)=-2 & \\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{2}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{2}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =(-1)\\cdot 3+4\\cdot (-5)+(-2)\\cdot 4=-31\\ne 0 & \\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{3}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$ We do not need to test ${v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the listed.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7977ec2e-bf6e-54d9-95ee-2c321a3e5408"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7977ec2e-bf6e-54d9-95ee-2c321a3e5408" :answer-field/id #uuid "7977ec2e-bf6e-54d9-95ee-2c321a3e5408" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f22b2e80-2bb5-5d03-bbb9-54a2fea7a7cf" :answer-field/choices ["answer-059410e3-40e1-5548-b990-cb95702ffd3d" "answer-9c557d7d-9732-552d-9575-c8050c68bf6e" "answer-c74f6ea3-5aeb-5209-af1b-0ceb2195f015" "answer-d76919dc-09b9-5af9-ab5a-dfa77d8e2c19" "answer-f22b2e80-2bb5-5d03-bbb9-54a2fea7a7cf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-059410e3-40e1-5548-b990-cb95702ffd3d" :answer/id #uuid "059410e3-40e1-5548-b990-cb95702ffd3d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9c557d7d-9732-552d-9575-c8050c68bf6e" :answer/id #uuid "9c557d7d-9732-552d-9575-c8050c68bf6e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c74f6ea3-5aeb-5209-af1b-0ceb2195f015" :answer/id #uuid "c74f6ea3-5aeb-5209-af1b-0ceb2195f015" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${v}_{2}$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d76919dc-09b9-5af9-ab5a-dfa77d8e2c19" :answer/id #uuid "d76919dc-09b9-5af9-ab5a-dfa77d8e2c19" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${v}_{3}$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f22b2e80-2bb5-5d03-bbb9-54a2fea7a7cf" :answer/id #uuid "f22b2e80-2bb5-5d03-bbb9-54a2fea7a7cf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186395898 :question/problem "Find a basis of ${H}^{⊥}$ if  $H=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0$ and $x\\cdot {h}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0 \\\\ x\\cdot {h}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd one), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the  second equation we get ${x}_{2}=0$ and then from the first equation we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{3}.$ So, any vector in ${H}^{⊥}$ has the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a basis of ${H}^{⊥}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186395899 :answer-field/correct "answer-a616221f-a3ff-597c-a09b-75e9b404d12d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a616221f-a3ff-597c-a09b-75e9b404d12d" "answer-555c54d9-1d37-5e6e-b4d1-bbbcb88d3fd6" "answer-e734f85d-d623-599c-bde5-4a3a67b9c408" "answer-a4541d70-89bc-50aa-b47d-4de40d32cc70" "answer-b92e104b-6b55-5959-8c95-c1a99a06687e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a616221f-a3ff-597c-a09b-75e9b404d12d" :answer/id #uuid "a616221f-a3ff-597c-a09b-75e9b404d12d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-555c54d9-1d37-5e6e-b4d1-bbbcb88d3fd6" :answer/id #uuid "555c54d9-1d37-5e6e-b4d1-bbbcb88d3fd6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e734f85d-d623-599c-bde5-4a3a67b9c408" :answer/id #uuid "e734f85d-d623-599c-bde5-4a3a67b9c408" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a4541d70-89bc-50aa-b47d-4de40d32cc70" :answer/id #uuid "a4541d70-89bc-50aa-b47d-4de40d32cc70" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b92e104b-6b55-5959-8c95-c1a99a06687e" :answer/id #uuid "b92e104b-6b55-5959-8c95-c1a99a06687e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-fc37a677-a8ea-5147-882f-3adaa2a20db5" :question/id #uuid "fc37a677-a8ea-5147-882f-3adaa2a20db5" :question/math-academy-id "q-39634" :question/problem "Find a basis of ${H}^{⊥}$, if $H=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0$ and $x\\cdot {h}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, we need to solve the following system: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0 \\\\ x\\cdot {h}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd one), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the  second equation we get ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{8}{x}_{3}$ and then from the first equation we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{8}{x}_{3}.$ So, any vector in ${H}^{⊥}$ has the form   \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{8}{x}_{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{8}{x}_{3} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{8} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{8} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a basis of ${H}^{⊥}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-00e407dd-b482-502c-8772-c1b38d73f7b0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-00e407dd-b482-502c-8772-c1b38d73f7b0" :answer-field/id #uuid "00e407dd-b482-502c-8772-c1b38d73f7b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4ba56a09-8a82-5e35-8607-9429ad0df8ec" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5b0019d1-7d0c-5a56-8b1c-2f7a6788a5d2" "answer-1879dc64-b7c0-542f-9bb2-e3524feeea44" "answer-e4290193-3591-5159-80ee-76f9d3d6cd1f" "answer-eb18dc94-7425-51f5-b83f-0fc3064bd2b7" "answer-4ba56a09-8a82-5e35-8607-9429ad0df8ec"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5b0019d1-7d0c-5a56-8b1c-2f7a6788a5d2" :answer/id #uuid "5b0019d1-7d0c-5a56-8b1c-2f7a6788a5d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1879dc64-b7c0-542f-9bb2-e3524feeea44" :answer/id #uuid "1879dc64-b7c0-542f-9bb2-e3524feeea44" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4290193-3591-5159-80ee-76f9d3d6cd1f" :answer/id #uuid "e4290193-3591-5159-80ee-76f9d3d6cd1f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eb18dc94-7425-51f5-b83f-0fc3064bd2b7" :answer/id #uuid "eb18dc94-7425-51f5-b83f-0fc3064bd2b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4ba56a09-8a82-5e35-8607-9429ad0df8ec" :answer/id #uuid "4ba56a09-8a82-5e35-8607-9429ad0df8ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{8} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135043 :tutorial/content "If a vector $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to all the vectors in a set $W,$ we say that $\\mathbf{u}$ is **orthogonal to $W,$** and we write $\\mathbf{u} \\perp W.$\n\nFor example, consider the set of vectors ![](images/b9/b986146bf3d9edbf85c9047aa0e0acac54b9dd962c5c1f1b2d1612a36444337b.svg) Can we find a *non-zero* vector ![](images/2b/2be010cc1a39d762cb78272a1c6f8b10574823fa549ba2e54066f15c1d312ff9.svg) that is orthogonal to both vectors in this set?\n\nWe know that if such a vector $\\mathbf u$ does exist, then the dot product of $\\mathbf u$ with each vector in the set will be zero:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo let's calculate these dot products:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find the components of $\\mathbf{u},$ we just need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the system's matrix is already in echelon form and has $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). So, $x_3$ is a free variable. \nNow, from the second equation, we have $x_2=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain $x_1=0.$ \n\nSetting $x_3=1,$ we get the vector\n\n![](images/4a/4aee36f32445ae5fe5286d7a5aa7600f1f7fc8449fb981509cbd999822408c90.svg)\n\nSo, we have found a vector $\\mathbf{u}$ that is orthogonal to both $\\mathbf{w}_1$ and $\\mathbf{w}_2.$\n\n![](images/a6/a64d75a0f21a4238c4b297f890d4a3202917d831c9d80427cffcc75dcdf343a0.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135044 :tutorial/content "Now, let's consider a *subspace* $H.$ The set containing all vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ that are orthogonal to $H$ is called the **orthogonal complement** of $H,$ and we denote it by $H^{\\perp}.$\n\nFor example, the orthogonal complement $H^\\perp$ of the 2D-plane $H$ passing through the origin in the three-dimensional space $\\mathbb{R}^3$ (shown below) is the line that passes through the origin and is perpendicular to the plane.\n\n![](images/7e/7eb817f84f5a9f642b5721559f41c2c99a44f234cd22b8cf6e820b7f5d61e262.png)\n\n  \n\nHow can we check whether a vector $\\mathbf v$ belongs to $H^{\\perp}?$ Sometimes, $H$ can contain many vectors (often infinitely many), so it may not be possible to check that $\\mathbf v$ is orthogonal to every individual vector in $H.$\n\nTo check whether a vector belongs to $H^{\\perp},$ we use the following theorem:\n\n*A vector $\\mathbf{v}$ belongs to $H^{\\perp}$ if it is orthogonal to a set of vectors that spans $H$. *\n\nIn other words:\n\n*If $H=\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{h}_1,\\mathbf{h}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{h}_n\\},$ then $\\mathbf{v} \\in H^{\\perp}$ if and only if $\\mathbf{v}$ is orthogonal to $\\{ \\mathbf{h}_1,\\mathbf{h}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{h}_n\\}.$*\n\nFor example, if the 2D-plane $H$ passing through the origin in the three-dimensional space $\\mathbb{R}^3$ (shown below) is spanned by the vectors $\\mathbf{h}_1$ and $\\mathbf{h}_2,$ then the orthogonal complement $H^\\perp$ of $H$ consists of all vectors that are perpendicular to both $\\mathbf{h}_1$ and $\\mathbf{h}_2.$\n\n![](images/f8/f8cf96c9871966fb0a7a921b70229e4a17bb9f6dad1756986f0500c70be6ab95.png)\n\n  \n\nSome other important things to notice about $H^{\\perp}$ are as follows:\n\n- $H^{\\perp}$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$\n\n- $(H^{\\perp})^{\\perp} = H$\n\n- $H \\cap H^{\\perp} = \\{\\mathbf 0\\}$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133989 :activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
